# KoreForge New Project / Repo Skill

## When to use

- Creating a new C# package under `eco-system/` or a tool under `tools/`.
- Creating a new Vue/npm package under `eco-web/`.
- Creating a new `Event.*` app or library under `event/`.
- Wiring an existing folder into the workspace runner, NuGet feed, and GitHub.

For repo-internal script details see [koreforge-builder-scripts](../koreforge-builder-scripts/SKILL.md). For Docker infra see [koreforge-docker-infra](../koreforge-docker-infra/SKILL.md).

## Choose ownership and location

| Kind | Folder | Naming | GitHub owner |
|---|---|---|---|
| Runtime KoreForge package | `eco-system/` | `KoreForge.<Area>` | `koreforger/KoreForge.<Area>` |
| Developer tool | `tools/` | `KoreForge.<Area>` | `koreforger/KoreForge.<Area>` |
| Vue / npm package | `eco-web/` | `KoreForge.<Area>.Vue` (or no suffix for shells) | `koreforger/<Name>` |
| Event app / library | `event/` | `Event.<Area>` | `koreforger/Event.<Area>` |

Never put product code at the workspace root.

## Hard rules (reject any plan that violates these)

- **`PackageReference` only.** Never add a `<ProjectReference>` to any `.csproj` in this workspace, even temporarily.
- **No vendoring.** Never copy another package's source into this repo to avoid a re-pack/restore loop. Pack the producer to `artifacts/packages` and consume the `.nupkg`.
- **Generated output → `artifacts/`.** No persistent `bin`, `obj`, `out`, `TestResults`, `coverage`, `packages`, `zips` in the repo root. Use `Get-KoreForgeRepoArtifactPath` from `koreforge-build.psm1`.
- **One top-level type per file.** Class/record/enum/interface/struct — one per `.cs`. The filename matches the type name.
- **Branches:** `main` (stable) and `development` (active). Work happens on `development` and feature branches; `main` is what publish workflows tag.
- **Naming consistency:** before naming a new file, look at existing files of the same kind in the workspace and match the established word order (`build-test.ps1` ↔ `build-coverage.ps1`, never `coverage-build.ps1`; `publish-nuget.yml` ↔ `publish-npm.yml`, never `npm-publish.yml`).

## Standard package repo layout

Use [eco-system/KoreForge.Web](eco-system/KoreForge.Web) as the reference template.

```
eco-system/KoreForge.<Area>/
  .git/
  .github/
    workflows/
      ci.yml                     ← build + test + coverage on every push/PR
      publish-nuget.yml          ← triggered by tag KoreForge.<Area>/v*
  .gitignore
  Directory.Build.props          ← MinVer + package metadata + IncludeSymbols
  Directory.Packages.props       ← central versions for all transitive packages
  NuGet.config                   ← only nuget.org (workspace adds KoreForgeLocal)
  KoreForge.<Area>.slnx          ← solution file
  README.md                      ← required (packed into nupkg)
  LICENSE.md                     ← required (packed into nupkg)
  doc/
    specification.md             ← what the library is and why
    developer-guide.md           ← how a consumer uses it
  scr/
    koreforge-build.psm1         ← copy from another repo unchanged
    build-clean.ps1
    build-rebuild.ps1
    build-test.ps1
    build-test-codecoverage.ps1
    build-pack.ps1
    git-create-repo.ps1          ← one-time: gh repo create
    github-set-nuget-secret.ps1  ← one-time: gh secret set NUGET_API_KEY
    release-nuget-from-github.ps1
    release-nuget-from-local.ps1
  src/
    KoreForge.<Area>.<Sub>/      ← one folder per packed assembly
      KoreForge.<Area>.<Sub>.csproj
      buildTransitive/           ← optional: transitive .targets included in nupkg
  tst/
    KoreForge.<Area>.Tests/
      KoreForge.<Area>.Tests.csproj
    KoreForge.<Area>.<Sub>.Tests/
    KoreForge.<Area>.Sample/      ← optional sample app, not packed
  coverlet.runsettings           ← required for coverage script
```

`tools/` repos are identical except they often produce `.exe`/global tool packages instead of libraries. `eco-web/` uses `package.json`/`pnpm-workspace.yaml` instead of `slnx`/`Directory.Build.props`. `event/` apps follow the same shape but are usually not published — they have no `publish-nuget.yml`.

## `Directory.Build.props` essentials

Mirror [eco-system/KoreForge.Web/Directory.Build.props](eco-system/KoreForge.Web/Directory.Build.props):

```xml
<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Authors>KoreForge</Authors>
    <Company>KoreForge</Company>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/koreforger/KoreForge.<Area></RepositoryUrl>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/koreforger/KoreForge.<Area></PackageProjectUrl>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <PackageLicenseFile>LICENSE.md</PackageLicenseFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <MinVerTagPrefix>KoreForge.<Area>/v</MinVerTagPrefix>
    <MinVerAutoIncrement>minor</MinVerAutoIncrement>
    <MinVerDefaultPreReleaseIdentifiers>alpha.0</MinVerDefaultPreReleaseIdentifiers>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(IsPackable)' != 'false'">
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <IncludeSymbols>true</IncludeSymbols>
    <SymbolPackageFormat>snupkg</SymbolPackageFormat>
    <PackageOutputPath>$(RepositoryRoot)artifacts\</PackageOutputPath>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="MinVer" PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
```

`MinVerTagPrefix` **must** be `KoreForge.<Area>/v` — the GitHub Actions tag trigger and the workspace release scripts both depend on this exact format. Never use plain `v*`.

## CI workflow ([.github/workflows/ci.yml](.github/workflows/ci.yml))

Use [eco-system/KoreForge.Web/.github/workflows/ci.yml](eco-system/KoreForge.Web/.github/workflows/ci.yml) verbatim. It runs on every branch push and PR (excluding tags), uses `actions/setup-dotnet@v4` with `dotnet-version: '10.0.x'`, builds the `slnx`, runs `dotnet test` with cobertura coverage, generates an HTML report via `reportgenerator`, and uploads both as artifacts. **Important:** `fetch-depth: 0` is required because MinVer reads git history.

## Publish workflow ([.github/workflows/publish-nuget.yml](.github/workflows/publish-nuget.yml))

Use [eco-system/KoreForge.Web/.github/workflows/publish-nuget.yml](eco-system/KoreForge.Web/.github/workflows/publish-nuget.yml) as the template. Must:

- Trigger only on `tags: ['KoreForge.<Area>/v*']` — repo-prefixed tags.
- Verify the tag is on `main`: `git merge-base --is-ancestor "$GITHUB_SHA" origin/main`. Refuse to publish from any other branch.
- Build `Release`, run tests `--no-build`, then `dotnet pack <each-packable.csproj> --no-build -o artifacts/`.
- `dotnet nuget push artifacts/*.nupkg --api-key "${{ secrets.NUGET_API_KEY }}" --source https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json --skip-duplicate`.
- Create a GitHub Release with `softprops/action-gh-release@v2`, files = `artifacts/*.nupkg`, `generate_release_notes: true`.

The `NUGET_API_KEY` repo secret is set by [scr/github-set-nuget-secret.ps1](scr/github-set-nuget-secret.ps1) once at repo creation.

## Per-repo NuGet.config

Per-repo files list **only** `nuget.org`:

```xml
<configuration>
  <packageSources>
    <clear />
    <add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" protocolVersion="3" />
  </packageSources>
</configuration>
```

The workspace's [NuGet.config](NuGet.config) adds `KoreForgeLocal → artifacts/packages` with package source mapping. This means: built standalone, the repo restores from nuget.org; built inside the workspace, it resolves `KoreForge.*`/`Event.*` from the local feed.

## End-to-end checklist for adding a new repo

1. **Create the folder and code**
   - `mkdir eco-system/KoreForge.<Area>` (or `tools/`, `event/`, `eco-web/` as appropriate).
   - Copy `Directory.Build.props`, `Directory.Packages.props`, `NuGet.config`, `coverlet.runsettings`, and `scr/` from a reference repo.
   - Update `MinVerTagPrefix`, `RepositoryUrl`, `PackageProjectUrl` to match the new package name.
   - Create `<Area>.slnx`, `src/KoreForge.<Area>/KoreForge.<Area>.csproj`, `tst/KoreForge.<Area>.Tests/KoreForge.<Area>.Tests.csproj`.
   - Add `README.md` and `LICENSE.md` (referenced by the props file).
   - Add `doc/specification.md` and `doc/developer-guide.md`.

2. **Wire into the workspace**
   - Append the workspace-relative path to the matching group in [builder.config.json](builder.config.json).
   - Verify `pwsh -File builder.ps1` shows the new repo with its actions.
   - Run `pwsh -File <repo>/scr/build-test.ps1` — must pass. Verify nothing was written to the repo root (`bin/`, `obj/` should be under `artifacts/repos/<repo>/build/`).
   - Run `pwsh -File <repo>/scr/build-pack.ps1 -Version 0.0.1-alpha.0` — `.nupkg` must land under `artifacts/repos/<repo>/packages/` (or `artifacts/` per `PackageOutputPath`).
   - Run `pwsh -File scr/pack-local-feed.ps1` — `.nupkg` must promote to `artifacts/packages/` so consumers can restore it.

3. **Create the GitHub repo**
   - `pwsh -File <repo>/scr/git-create-repo.ps1` — runs `git init`, initial commit, `gh repo create koreforger/KoreForge.<Area> --public --source . --remote origin --push`.
   - Create the `development` branch and push: `git checkout -b development && git push -u origin development`.
   - Set the default GitHub branch to `main` (already is) and protect both branches as your team standard requires.
   - `pwsh -File <repo>/scr/github-set-nuget-secret.ps1` — sets `NUGET_API_KEY`.

4. **Verify CI**
   - Push a trivial change to `development`. Confirm `.github/workflows/ci.yml` runs green and uploads `test-results` + `coverage-report` artifacts.

5. **Test publish (optional but recommended)**
   - From `main`: `pwsh -File <repo>/scr/release-nuget-from-github.ps1 -Version 0.0.1-alpha.0`.
   - This creates and pushes the tag `KoreForge.<Area>/v0.0.1-alpha.0`.
   - The publish workflow runs, packs, pushes to NuGet.org with `--skip-duplicate`, and creates a GitHub Release.

6. **Add the package to the central versions file**
   - Once published (even as alpha), other workspace repos can pin it via [Directory.Packages.props](eco-system/KoreForge.Web/Directory.Packages.props) and consume from the local feed during dev.

## Standard versioning

| Phase | Version | How to publish |
|---|---|---|
| Local dev | `1.0.1-alpha` (or whatever `builder.config.json:packageVersion` says) | `pack-local-feed.ps1` — never reaches nuget.org |
| First public alpha | `0.1.0-alpha.0` | tag `KoreForge.<Area>/v0.1.0-alpha.0` on `main` |
| Subsequent alphas | bump pre-release: `0.1.0-alpha.1`, `0.1.0-alpha.2` | re-tag |
| Stable | `0.1.0` | tag `KoreForge.<Area>/v0.1.0` on `main` |
| Coordinated workspace release | All KoreForge.* packages on the same `MAJOR.MINOR` | use [scr/release-nuget-from-github.ps1](scr/release-nuget-from-github.ps1) |

`MinVer` uses the closest matching tag for the current commit. Untagged commits get `<latest>+<height>-alpha.0.<sha>`.

## Validation checklist

```powershell
# No project references anywhere
rg "<ProjectReference" -g "*.csproj"
# No vendored KoreForge source
rg "KoreForge\." -g "*.cs" -l | Select-String "vendor|copied"
# Repo discovers in builder
pwsh -File builder.ps1
# Per-repo build clean
pwsh -File <repo>/scr/build-test.ps1
pwsh -File <repo>/scr/build-pack.ps1 -Version 0.0.1-alpha.0
# Local feed promotion works
pwsh -File scr/pack-local-feed.ps1
# Workspace cleanup leaves nothing in the repo
pwsh -File build.ps1 -CleanArtifacts
git status   # repo should be clean
```

## When to add an Event app instead of a KoreForge package

Pick `event/Event.<Area>` (not `eco-system/KoreForge.<Area>`) when the code is **product-specific** and not a reusable building block. Event apps:

- Are not published to NuGet (no `publish-nuget.yml`).
- Reference KoreForge packages via `PackageReference`, never `ProjectReference`.
- Live in their own `event/Event.<Area>/` folder with the same `scr/` script set so the runner picks them up.
- May host Docker-dependent integration tests — see [koreforge-docker-infra](../koreforge-docker-infra/SKILL.md).
